This code is to be installed on the Daisy Patch Eurorack Module.
It functions similar to an Eventide H-910 Harmonizer and Delay

This code utilizes the DaisyDuino.h and U8g2lib.h libraries

The controls:
CRTL 1: Delay time
CTRL 2: Feedback
CTRL 3: Dry signal level
CTRL 4: Wet signal level

Encoder (IN PITCH SHIFT MODE): Changes pitch shift ratio (+ or - 10 cents)
Encoder (IN DELAY ONLY MODE): Shifts right delay line time by + or - 250 memory addresses per turn
Encoder push: changes back and forth from pitch shifter mode to delay only mode. (default is pitch shift mode)

Both the left and right audio channels have their own delay lines in both modes
The left channel is the pitch shifted channel, the right channel is delay only (in pitch shift mode)
In pitch shift mode the delay times are set to the same time for each channel,
However, in delay only mode you can offset the right delay line from the left delay line's time to achieve two different delay times

Audio channels:
IN 1 (left, pitch shifted and delayed when in pitch shift mode)
IN 2 (right, delay only)

OUT 1 (left, pitch shifted and delay in pitch shift mode)
OUT 2 (right, delay only)

patch notes:
1.1: Cleaned up code a tiny bit
1.2: added OLED functionality and ability to offset right delay line in delay only mode
1.3: added organic pitch shifting. Used to be locked to only shifting in semitone increments, now you can shift up by + or - 10 cents with each increment of the encoder
    ****NOTE***** YOU MUST CHANGE YOUR PITCHSHIFTER.H FILE IN YOUR DAISYDUINO LIBRARY FOLDER TO UTILIZE THIS CODE
add the following function to your pitchshifter.h file:

    void SetCents(float cents)
    {
        float ratio = powf(2.0f, cents / 1200.0f);

        // Determine shift direction
        if(ratio >= 1.0f)
            shift_up_ = true;
        else
            shift_up_ = false;

        // Use signed mod_freq to allow negative shifts
        mod_freq_ = ((ratio - 1.0f) * sr_) / del_size_;

        // Don't clamp negative values; the Phasor handles direction
        phs_[0].SetFreq(fabsf(mod_freq_));
        phs_[1].SetFreq(fabsf(mod_freq_));
    }

